Reproduced the problem. section_b(findings) never receives the note: it compares the record with copies held in EXPECT, and only section A parses the note's two tables. On a mirror of the repo (work/literature-check/verify-custom/skeptic-coherence/), I planted four wrong prose counts in the note: 288 findings, 33 agents, Five of seven, 66 confirmed and 5 in part. The current gate still exited 0 with 515 PASS and printed 'OK — the literature-check note matches its record: verdicts, counts, quotations and citations' (planted_unpatched.out).

These statements therefore overstate the check:
- note header lines 7-9: 'checks the note's ... main counts against the stored record ... on every run of the gate';
- CLAUDE.md:36 and :206: 'counts';
- review/literature-check/README.md:5-7: 'checks the note against ... the workflow results';
- wiki/method/literature-check.md:16: 'main counts'.
Only the-gate.md:53 gives the true scope. The second review's wiki LC-5 had already flagged the header ('overstates it the same way'). The main session's change of 'counts' to 'main counts' did not fix it.

I keep the verifier's fix, strengthening the gate rather than narrowing five descriptions, with three revisions:
- I added the phrase 'one of ten anchors', so that the wiki's 'each count' holds for every count section B names.
- I rewrote the-gate.md. The verifier's 'or to one of the main counts ...' was vague and attached awkwardly.
- I changed CLAUDE.md 'counts' to 'main counts' twice. Prose counts such as 'Nine papers' and the 26 OpenAlex queries stay unchecked, as iron-rule.md:60 says.

